f the the results of that work  was here you see  a graph that shows you the accumulated  light conditions since seeding on the  horizontal axis on the vertical axis you  see the shoot dry mass or shoot dry  weight and you get a very nice linear  line not surprising when you think about  a crop like lettuce that is a vegetative  crop  so there's not really all that much  significant about this graph except for  the fact that you're looking at 35 data  points and each of those data points was  a single treatment and each treatment  started with about 800 seats  so the the amount of work that went into  giving you this graph this this very  nice linear relationship  was quite significant and many people  were involved in this effort and it took  many many  hours of work to to get all these data  points for this graph  another thing we looked at was was leaf  area versus  the horizontal axis  shoot fresh mass  and on the other  on the right hand side on the vertical  axis we looked at the dry mass so we got  nice correlations between leaf area  and and  shoot fresh mass as well as the dry mass  and the fresh mass  again not particularly surprising except  i want to mention that if you look at  the blue line you see a very nice linear  relationship between dry mass and fresh  mass and this resulted very quickly in  significant discussions with  particularly with professor langhans  because the staff obviously was more  interested in taking the fresh mass and  not do the  tedious dry weight measurements dry mass  measurements  but he stood his grounds and we lost out  and so we did  continue  uh weighing uh plants after they went  through the drying cycle and and  we had to do a lot of extra work because  of that but  to his credit that also meant that we  had better results in the end and more  publishable results  when we were done  so everything was going nice and  smoothly until we hit a significant  problem and the problem was called  tipwork  and tipburn is a physi