ws of lettuce plants and we we  grew the plants and looked at the  results and much to our surprise the the  idea worked perfectly in in fact the  plants that received additional air  supply  with this system did not get any tip  burn where neighboring plants did have  significant amount of tipper  and so  after we saw the result ourselves we  could  report back to professor langest when he  came back from his trip or from his  vacation  uh that indeed we discovered that this  was a great idea and we should  investigate more  and so he then quickly told us then  to use turbulator fans overhead fans  that  could blow large amounts of air  vertically down on the lettuce crop to  help increase plant transpiration and  overcome this this dipper and issue  and so this is what we we started doing  and this is how we overcome the typical  issue issue but to a certain extent  and at certain extent this i'm trying to  show you here in this graph where on the  horizontal axis you look at dry mass  accumulation on the vertical axis you  look at water use we carefully tracked  water use of plants in our system  and we could come up with  these lines and these are  regression lines so they're not the  actual data but the result of the the  data that we collected to try to  polish up the graph a little bit but you  can see here there's different  light treatments that have different  rates of water use  as the plants are growing through the  different stages to final harvest  and so we surmised from this craft that  that you needed to have a minimum rate  of water use or plant transpiration  in order to overcome this tip burn  problem you can see in the top of the  slide that treatments up to 16 moles of  light or so did not  suffer from tip burn where treatments  above that 20 and 22 moles in the bottom  did receive or did experience tip burn  issues  so this is why  if you've been at all familiar with the  cea program this is why  we typically recommend that 16 to 70  moles of 