n by trading we  discovered that within limits  you could decide instead of adding more  supplemental lighting which is very  expensive you could you could add or  increase the co2 concentration and get  the same end result  and so this was an interesting  discovery and and i think when you think  about commercial production this can  have some significant implications  because obviously it will be cheaper to  add co2 compared to adding supplemental  lighting  and then all that work resulted in a set  of growth curves we use the second order  exponential polynomial you see the  equation in the box upright  where we only have one  variable the the daily light integral  the amount of light we provided on a  daily basis to the plants that we  provide that light every day to try to  keep the plants at their fixed growing  rate to make sure we reach the target  harvest date  and you can see here for different daily  light integrals what the growth curve  looked like  for the varieties that we were using at  the time which was austenita bib lettuce  we also looked at efficiency  rates uh so how much energy do you need  to provide and what do you get in return  in terms of biomass  and more recently i found a paper by  monteith in that from 1977 that looked  at  efficiency rates in terms of the amount  of light it was intercepted versus the  dry matter production  um  and he looked at at outdoor crops he  looked at apples barley potatoes and  sugar beets and you see these colored  lines here  indicating  the data that he found  and then from this data i can calculate  an average efficiency taking too into  account that the heat of combustion for  dry matter is approximately 7.5  kilojoules per gram so i could look at  the energy that you provided through  sunlight versus the energy that you you  gain from  dry matter production and then you can  calculate an efficiency  and then the little blue line  towards the origin of the graph here is  is the line for our lettuce plants  and now you think that it looks like the  lettuce is not doing so well but you  have to keep in mind that the lettuce  crops that we were growing only required  a 24 day time period in the greenhouse  we grew it for 11 days in the growth  chamber and then 24 more days in the  in the  greenhouse so after  35 days or five weeks the plants were  finished and we reached the 150 gram  fresh matter  freshwater  target harvest size  so if you if you think about this the  outdoor crops can be grown only once in  outdoor season but the greenhouse crops  can be grown actually 15 times in the  same area in a greenhouse so multiply  both axis by 15 and you see that  the greenhouse crop was doing  significantly better than the field  crops  as i mentioned we did lots of  experiments  some were funny and some were  informative one of them that i liked  particularly  was this continuous production in this  case in nft or nutrient film technique  showing you the visual growth curve i  showed you the lines that we that we  calculated from the data that we  collected here you see actually in a  visual representation an actual  representation of what that growth curve  looks like so every day we would plant a  new row and move the rows up until we  finally ended up with a complete  picture of what every stage of the the  production looked like  we did the same for floating  systems  in the meantime we had visited a grower  in canada that was using these floating  systems and we we quickly realized the  benefits of this system  and and so we started working  on floating systems as well and here you  see the same idea  a visual representation of the growth  curve of lettuce plants in that floating  system  and then eventually and i'm sure  everybody here is familiar with us we  were able to and i think i still think  this is very remarkable out of the  research that we did  and this is to the credit of professors  uh langhans and and albright  they were able to convince our our  sponsors that it was a good idea to  build a demonstration facility and i  thought i don't think there are many  uh research projects that ultimately end  up in a significantly scaled  demonstration facility in which we were  going to demonstrate that this lattice  production system was going to work out  and that at that stage  the professor retired and i and i left  cornell  for for rutgers university so i'm  switching now to uh some of the work  that i've been involved with at rutgers  university and you quickly can see that  i'm switching to a totally different  crop  rutgers is is more known for their work  on tomatoes so when i came there i had  to switch my thinking from a  vegetative crop to a flowering crop like  like tomatoes and this is not a  particularly good picture of a tomato  plant but it has one feature that i  wanted you to look at which is the fact  that the stem here  is is cut and and the ruckus approach  was  a different approach for the traditional  tomato production system in the sense  that they decided to grow single cluster  or dual cluster  tomato plants and that meant that after  the first fruit cluster had started to  develop they would break off the  the growing tip  and and let the plant put all its  resources into  into  the few fruit that it was developing  before the plant was then discarded so  the cropping cycle was much  reduced in terms of time  but also the spacing was much increased  we could pack these plants much closer  together  in the system that we were looking at at  least theoretically it appeared that a  system like this would be as productive  as the more traditional lean and lower  system that is that you can see in most  commercial greenhouse operations  so i'm switching to a tomato crop  some of the early work was funded by  nasa  and this is also a bit of a contentious  point uh with the cornell group here  at the time uh rutgers had a an end  scored project and and  nasa funded project  where cornell also uh  was