ere not done very  extensively certainly not on tomato  crops  and then by talking to our specialists  on tomato crops we knew that  the environmental conditions  environmental conditions during  flowering and fruit set were really  important and that starts around 55 days  after seeding for the particular system  that we were using  so those conditions if you if you do  perturbations in that time period and  that for our case it was about a  two-week time period then that can have  significant impact on on the quality and  the and the yield of the plants  and we also looked at harvesting the  plants at the typical breaker stage  which for a tomato plant means that the  color is just changing from green to to  slightly orange or pinkish  and as harvesting at a later date  because we felt and we had indications  that if you wait longer  you get better qualities aspects of your  of your fruit production and so indeed  as you can see on the bottom point here  we did find that there were significant  improvements if you wait longer  before you start harvesting the fruit  which is obviously not a major  surprise but it was it was nice that we  could now  have a quantitative way of of showing  this  so the work was done in growth chambers  mostly  so very familiar to the growth chambers  that you use at cornell here  we used a lot of mylar screening around  our plant stands to try to overcome the  issue of sight lighting so typically you  would have sight lighting issues and you  would remove  plants on the outside rows from your  experiments but since we had limited  area we wanted to include those plants  so we used those screens to to try to  overcome the side light issue  and  the way we did it we started plants all  in the same growth chamber and then  depending on the treatment they were  moved to different uh growth chambers to  to receive their treatment so of course  there are issues associated with when  you start moving plants you you disturb  them you stress t