s to try to  to not duplicate efforts because i think  some of that is going on at these  institutions  when i came to rutgers i inherited  a greenhouse shell so it wasn't looking  as nice as it looks in this picture  my predecessor was able to get funding  for the framework of a open roof  greenhouse system which is a newer  design that allows for  large amounts of air exchange to try to  keep the temperature inside pretty much  the same as outdoor conditions in the  summertime  which is typically not feasible when you  use mechanical ventilation systems  but if you have these open roof  greenhouses you can  get to  those conditions  so we  quickly  realized we needed to improve this  facility to get it up to a research  great facility and and gene reese  my graduate student and um technician at  the time  uh  did the yeoman's work of getting this  facility in great shape  to do experimentation and as you can see  from the the reference here he also  looked into  a a heating system a floor heating  system  uh and look at the benefits of floor  heating for for crop production  so here you see the inside of greenhouse  it's a an ebon flood floor system which  is a very nice way of irrigating your  crop if you can grow them in pots and  sitting directly on the floor  and then embedded in that concrete layer  we installed a heating system a a  plastic pipe system through which we  pump uh hot water  and the way that system was designed is  shown in this graphic so you have your  concrete slab you have the heating pipes  and you have also the the the supply and  drainage system for your irrigation  system that ends up in an underground  storage tank  where you store your nutrient solution  to be ready for the next  irrigation cycle  so the top of the floor was carefully  designed so it had a good drainage  system we call it a w shape we had two  rows of holes down the length of the the  bay  that collect the nutrient solution after  the irrigation cycle and then drains 