w for better control of water uptake  but nevertheless if you look at our  lettuce data  it requires two and a half liters of  water to grow one five ounce head of  lettuce so that's a significant amount  of water that we need  to grow as simple plant as lettuce  so the fact remains that we need large  quantities of water  to grow our crops whether we do it in a  greenhouse or not  greenhouse production comes a long way  again around the time that professor  langhans was born we we saw a lot of  cold frames  obviously that required a lot of labor  you see a large crew there working  in the netherlands we had  greenhouses specifically designed for  seed grapes so for for consumption  grapes  and you see also  some older greenhouses on the left hand  side for carnations and on the  right-hand side for tomato production  in a traditional old-fashioned system  when professor langhans came to cornell  in the early 50s  cornell looked something like this you  could still park your car in front of  willett straight and there was a parking  lot on the egg quad  by the way  ken poster was still working at the time  he see a picture of ken post  from 1951 so a year before professor  langhas came  came here and you see this the state of  the greenhouse  i have to add to that when i came in the  in the late 80s the greenhouse didn't  look much different  obviously  some of them some of them obviously some  of them have improved so talking about  parking  if you live in a in a densely populated  area  then you're dealing with this issue so  this is another example of the increase  in population growth and the challenges  that we are are facing uh we are faced  with  dealing with that increase  going back to the greenhouses we see  lots of  uh developments lots of technology that  that is in use today that really make  these very highly sophisticated and  high-tech systems  so just to name some examples you see  the greenhouse on the upper left-hand  side  a design that doesn't use any gutters  anymore the greenhouse is designed so  that the glass panes the very large  glass panes actually form or gutter  themselves  and the trusses are not running  across the base but are running in the  length of the bay actually supporting  the area where normally the gutter would  be and the trusses is the truss is  designed in such a way that it provides  a space for the energy current to be  stored in  again to try to maximize light  transmission through this system so  there's a very  uh  specific design features that allow for  maximum light transmission in this  highly modern greenhouse we have  sophisticated on the upper right hand  side we have sophisticated  irrigation systems here you see an  entire table being moved by a  computerized system to an irrigation  station the funnel there  would be supplied with the right amount  of water and nutrients for the  the gerbera crop to grow optimally in  this in this system so the the computer  decides when the crop needs irrigation  and then moves the table to an  irrigation station  to provide it with water and nutrients  we have very sophisticated robots we  call them flat fixtures the the that's  that that  make sure that every cell in a plug tray  is filled with a viable plant so there's  a camera system that inspects an entire  tray  a mechanical system that takes out the  tray the cells that were not germinated  or have diseased plants and then refills  those open spaces with  with viable plants to make sure that  when a grower sells a flat 100 of the  plants in that flat are actually viable  for the next stage of production and  then ultimately on the lower right hand  side we have high throughput greenhouses  in which plants are grown in individual  containers that are that are kept track  of by a computer system so these plants  can be manipulated individually  in the greenhouse system for  for measurements and and  and handling so  very expensive but highly  uh computerized and and  automated systems to to make sure we can  handle plants very efficiently without  uh much human interaction  so  to come to some conclusions um  i think to help feed  the growing world  to me it seems greenhouse food  production is a is going to be a  critical component in that  however i think  despite the need and i think the  demonstrated capabilities as i've tried  to indicate in this talk  there's there's  limited funding  and and also i think we have we we don't  have the support from college  administrators that itself could be an  entire presentation uh but uh i think i  think you understand what i'm talking  about  so what do we need to do i think i think  as a group as a as a group of scientists  working in controlled environments we  need to become stronger advocates for  greenhouse production i think we need to  find a way to tell our story better and  to tell it to people that really matter  and that can then take action to help us  with funding and help  further the cause  another area i think and i strongly  believe in this and where we can make  improvements is to to increase our  collaborations  locally regionally but also  internationally  and i think  you may have heard a lot of discussion  recently about vertical farming  um i don't necessarily think vertical  farming is a bad idea but i certainly  don't think it's a great idea  but i think we need to come up as a  group with a viable alternative  in addition and i get to back to  vertical farming in a second in addition  i think we need to look at our  inputs and we need to look at the  efficient use of our inputs we need to  reduce energy consumption  and waste we need to increase the  biodegradable materials the use of them  and we need more recycling in our  industry  we are working on those issues but i  don't think we we we do enough of it yet  because i think if we don't  outsiders will do it for us and of  course when outsiders start looking at  us  they may come up with different ideas  and different outcomes and outcomes that  may not be always in in the  in to the benefit of the greenhouse  industry  so we need to look at things like life  cycle assessments of our production  systems we know we use a lot of energy  we know significant resources  particularly water  and we need to look at the life cycle  assessment of those  production processes and include things  like carbon footprints and other  environmental impacts to come to a a  transparent and fair assessment of what  we're doing because i think  if we don't do that others will  and others may question why we are using  significant resources for example  to produce non-essentials and i think a  a rose crop in the greenhouse  or perhaps even a lettuce crop in the  greenhouse could be labeled by outsiders  as a non-essential crop  and i think if that happens  we're going to have a long way fighting  ourselves back and trying to make sure  that those those industries remain  viable  because i think if once we get into a  negative consumer  perception case  and there are examples of that in our  industry  if we get to that point it's going to be  very hard to  to fight those perceptions and to  to get back on top again  so back to vertical farming  i found this  reference and  some pictures  i don't think vertical farming is a new  idea here you see an idea by  a professor in australia in austria i'm  sorry  from the early 60s  looking at a conveyor system in a  vertical tower  trying to grow crops using supplemental  lighting to help  speed up production  so i think the vertical farming is not a  new  idea here you see a modern version of  what a vertical farm looked like in an  urban area so the idea is you produce it  close to to densely populated centers  you produce it in  a hydroponic system  but minimizing really the the  transportation distance  between where you produce it and where  it's consumed  so is it a good idea  you should ask professor albright to  give a talk because he can give a ve